Here's a complete example. I'll walk through each piece:

---

### 1. Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Create a new user in the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateUser(
        [Description("The user's full name")] string name,
        [Description("The user's email address")] string email,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, email });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/users", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = true), Description("List all users, optionally filtered by name.")]
    public async Task<string> ListUsers(
        [Description("Optional name filter (substring match)")] string? nameFilter = null,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var url = nameFilter is not null ? $"/users?name={nameFilter}" : "/users";
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

### 2. `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

### 3. How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services via primary constructor (shown above) |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services as method params — SDK resolves them automatically |

**Key rules:**
- `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, and `IProgress<>` are all **auto-injected** — they never appear in the tool's JSON schema shown to the LLM.
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM as tool inputs.
- Register services normally in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.) — the MCP SDK resolves them from the same DI container.

**Constructor injection** (as in `ApiTools` above) is preferred when a class has multiple tools sharing the same dependencies. **Method injection** works for simple static utility classes where each method might need different services.
